WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.575
Glacier Coolers just won a big order. Three thousand coolers, shipping next week. What could go wrong?

00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:12.900
This is order to cash: one order's life, from "they ordered" to "cash in the bank", when our client sells.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:19.675
Remember the restaurant. Our client is the kitchen. It confirms, cooks, serves, bills, and gets paid.

00:00:19.675 --> 00:00:26.075
Every step leaves a document. And every document comes from, or creates, a record in NetSuite.

00:00:26.075 --> 00:00:33.975
Step one. The retailer sends an eight fifty purchase order. Orderful checks it, and the connector creates a Sales Order.

00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:41.375
Every value must find a home. The P O number, the ship window, the distribution center address, and each item.

00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:48.400
If an item or address can't be matched, there is no Sales Order. And no Sales Order means nothing ships.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:55.626
Step two. The client confirms with an eight fifty five, built from the Sales Order. It says what will really ship.

00:00:55.626 --> 00:01:03.251
If the retailer changes the order, an eight sixty arrives. Apply it before the warehouse picks, or you ship the wrong thing.

00:01:03.251 --> 00:01:11.601
Step three. The warehouse picks and packs. In NetSuite, that is the Item Fulfillment, and it builds the eight fifty six ship notice.

00:01:11.601 --> 00:01:21.676
The eight fifty six is the hardest document. It must match the real boxes. Same quantities, same cartons, and every S S C C label barcode.

00:01:21.676 --> 00:01:29.326
A late or wrong ship notice is the most common chargeback, because the dock must open and count every box by hand.

00:01:29.326 --> 00:01:34.676
Step four. The client creates an Invoice in NetSuite, and that builds the eight ten.

00:01:34.676 --> 00:01:43.076
Invoice after the ship notice, only what shipped, at the P O price. A rejected invoice can delay cash by a month or two.

00:01:43.076 --> 00:01:49.651
Step five. Some partners send an eight twenty remittance, and finance applies it as a Customer Payment.

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:57.776
Many partners only use the core three. Eight fifty in, eight fifty six out, and eight ten out, plus nine ninety sevens.

00:01:57.776 --> 00:02:05.002
A real lesson. A test order arrived, it was valid in Orderful, and acknowledged in a minute. Then it just sat.

00:02:05.002 --> 00:02:15.177
Weeks later, NetSuite said the eight fifty was never enabled for that partner. Check the record exists in the E R P, not just that it's green in Orderful.

00:02:15.177 --> 00:02:20.727
Remember, valid does not mean delivered. Watch invoices as closely as orders.

00:02:20.727 --> 00:02:28.727
Order in, confirm out, ship out, bill out, money in. Each step must match the one before it, and every mismatch has a price.

00:02:28.727 --> 00:02:33.152
Next, we flip sides: procure to pay, when our client is the buyer.

